# Line endings.
#
# Development happens on Windows; CI runs on Linux. Without this, a file
# written by Python's text mode on Windows is stored with CRLF, CI regenerates
# it with LF, and `git diff --exit-code` reports every line as changed in a
# file nobody edited. That is exactly how the `fixtures` job failed on the
# first push.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` means: git decides what is text, and stores and checks it
# out with LF. Editors on Windows have handled LF for many years.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Generated and diffed by CI. Called out explicitly because this one is not a
# matter of taste: `scripts/capture_api_fixtures.py` writes it with an
# explicit LF newline, and the stored form has to agree.
apps/web/fixtures/api-payloads.json text eol=lf

# Lockfiles are generated. Marking them keeps merge conflicts readable and
# stops diffs being dominated by whitespace noise.
uv.lock            text eol=lf -diff
pnpm-lock.yaml     text eol=lf -diff

# Binary: never translate, never diff as text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.mp3  binary
*.wav  binary
*.m4a  binary
